<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">For a professional football club, sponsorship is the central source of income alongside match operations — and sponsorship depends directly on a direction that can be sold. The problem at DSC Arminia Bielefeld: the strongest, most emotional associations with the club arose uncontrolled, from the fans, not from a managed direction — “We are East Westphalia”, “Nobody conquers the Teutoburg Forest”, “stubborn, tenacious, combative”.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">These feelings are the club's real capital — no industrial company could produce them with an advertising budget. But this capital could not be invested: without a shared bracket, there is no way to explain to a sponsor what they are buying into. The task, then, was not to create emotion but to make the existing emotion manageable.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Before any design work, there were therefore workshops with the club and market research among fans and sponsors — one of the first professionally run brand projects for a Bundesliga club in Germany.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:760px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">“Movement” instead of random folklore</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The workshops and market research produced an overarching bracket that sponsors could buy into: “movement” — as the club's attitude, not just as an image. “Movement” takes up the existing fan associations — tenacity, getting up again, carrying on are all terms of movement — and at the same time connects them to communication, design and sponsorship offers. The gain lies not in replacing feeling with something more matter-of-fact, but in the fact that the same feeling now has a form that can be put in front of a board.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 4rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Logo and typeface: refinement instead of a fresh start</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Unlike Cobiax or Gneuss, no new symbol was needed here — the club crest was merely cleaned up and otherwise kept unchanged. What changed was the hierarchy: “DSC Arminia” — the distinctive element that defines the brand — moves dominantly to the fore. “Bielefeld” is set smaller, as a purely descriptive but necessary addition below the actual logo elements.</p>
  <div class="case-img reveal reveal-delay-1" style="background:var(--paper);margin-bottom:2rem;">
    <img src="/img/new-arminia-logo.svg" alt="DSC Arminia Bielefeld — logo with new hierarchy, cleaned-up crest and the Arminia house typeface" style="object-fit:contain;padding:8%;">
  </div>

  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A club does not communicate its direction once but thousands of times — on shirts, stadium boards, digital displays and merchandise, over years. With this breadth of use, a licensed existing typeface would either have caused ongoing licence costs or merely claimed “movement” without ever embodying it in the letterforms. That is why a dedicated, licence-free house typeface was created, exclusively for the club's communication.</p>
  <div class="reveal reveal-delay-2" style="background:var(--paper-2);padding:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2.25rem);margin-bottom:1rem;">
    <p class="font-arminia" style="font-size:clamp(1.1rem,2.2vw,1.6rem);line-height:1.5;color:var(--ink);">This typeface carries the brand value of movement into every piece of communication, with no ongoing licence costs. It exists only in the communication of DSC Arminia Bielefeld.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:.8125rem;color:rgba(var(--ink-rgb),.5);margin-bottom:2rem;">Rendered live in the purpose-built house typeface “Arminia” — not an image.</p>

  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Where there is no text, a third element carries the same idea further: a recurring, diagonally offset surface pattern for backgrounds, presentations and video transitions — the same movement, now without letters.</p>
  <div class="reveal reveal-delay-1" style="background:var(--paper-2);padding:1.5rem;">
    <img src="/img/arminia-bewegungselement.svg" alt="DSC Arminia Bielefeld — recurring surface pattern for backgrounds and presentations" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;">
  </div>
</section>

